Patch: SR x86-64 output support for the Redguard engine
Upstream: https://github.com/M-HT/SR at the commit pinned by SR_COMMIT in tools/pipeline.py, plus the OUTPUT_TYPE switch to OUT_X64 applied by pipeline.build().
Original: SR's x86-64 backend (SR/SR_full_x64_instr.c) does not translate BSF, JECXZ, JO, JNO, the word and dword forms of SCAS, or the integer MMX instructions (MOVD, MOVQ, PAND, PANDN, POR, PXOR, PADD*, PSUB*, shifts) that the Redguard engine uses. SR_basic.c lets a fixup alias force code analysis of a range the metadata marks as data.
Replacement: adds those instructions to the x64 translator (SCAS now covers all three widths with the repeat prefix mapped to the right form), makes a fixup alias respect the no-code list, and prints the address of an unsupported jump.
Effect: the full engine translates to x86-64 without hand-written stubs for these instructions. The generated assembly for a given input is deterministic; tools/pipeline.py compares it when this patch changes.
Why: stock SR targets 32-bit output; the native Windows and Linux builds are 64-bit.
Evidence: docs/architecture.md; the Wine and Linux runs record the translated engine executing.

